<%= h2 "Wrap" %>

The `Wrap()` macro is a convenient way to run one or more steps of an operation within your own code block, for instance, when a bunch of steps need to be wrapped in a database transaction.

<%= code "upload", collapse: :meths %>

In the above example, the steps `#upload` and `#transfer` are run through your wrap handler `MyTransaction`.

<%= h3 "Wrap handler" %>

The key idea is that a wrap handler can use `yield` to run the wrapped steps.

<%= code "my_transaction" %>

_# FIXME: ADD 2.1 EXAMPLE

A wrap handler can be any type of [callable] exposing the [circuit interface](/2.1/docs/operation/index.html#operation-internals-circuit-interface), meaning it receives three positional arguments and returns another set of three.

The `ctx` argument is the application `ctx` passed through the operation at the time *before* the Wrap step got called. The remaining two incoming arguments are library-specific.

<%= h4 "Yield" %>

Use `yield` to run the wrapped steps at any point in your handler code. This will invoke `update`, then `transfer` as if they were a separate mini-operation (actually, they are!).

<%= code :ruby do %>ctx, flow_options, signal = yield(ctx, flow_options, circuit_options)
<% end %>

Pass all three circuit interface arguments to `yield`, it runs the wrapped steps, and returns their return set: a new `ctx`, the library-specific `flow_options`, and the terminus `signal` of the execution of the wrapped steps.

To understand the returned `signal`, keep in mind that your wrapped sequence of steps is actually a real mini operation, and `yield` runs it. That's why the returned `signal` usually is a `success` or `failure` terminus signal from that wrapped "operation".

<%= image_tag "wrap.png" %>

The Wrap() macro automatically wires those signals accordingly.

<%= info do %>
Coming from TRB 2.1, you can still call `yield` without arguments. However, you will get a deprecation notice, please pass the three incoming circuit interface arguments to `yield` to reduce compatibility code.
<% end %>

<%= h4 "Return set" %>

Note that, from your handler, you always want to return the variables returned from `yield` (unless there was an exception in `yield`), otherwise you might lose `ctx` variables or library data, e.g. when tracing.

<%= code "yield-and-return" %>

You don't have to return the "native" signal from `yield`, you may return any signal you need to indicate the outcome to the outer world, but make sure it is properly wired.

See # FIXME for a better example.

<%= h3 "Transaction" %>

A very typical real-world use-case of Wrap() is when, well, wrapping a sequence of steps into a database transaction. This allows adding specific code for a rollback in case something on the database level failed. Hundreds of TRB users have code similar to the following.

<%= code "my_transaction", root: ".", file: "../trailblazer-rails/test/dummy/app/concepts/song/operation/upload.rb" %>

This might look a bit clumsy, but most of the code is very straight-forward.

1. Your main intent, wrapping a sequence of steps into an `ActiveRecord::Base.transaction` block, contains the `yield` invocation that runs the wrapped steps.
2. Having the `signal` of the wrapped activity returned, you can decide how you'd like to interpret that. Usually, looking at the signals `:semantic` field indicates the outcome.
3. In case you're not happy, a `raise ActiveRecord::Rollback` will make ActiveRecord undo whatever database commits happened in the block.
4. Instead of returning the nested activity's `signal`, which is completely legit, `Wrap()` also allows you to return a `Right` or `Left` signal to communicate the outcome of the entire `Wrap()` component.

